It seems the expected behavior for both `WriteLine` and `WriteLineAsync` is to _not_ indent the very first line. All other lines should be indented. Although it's not documented (we should fix that), the unit tests are specifically verifying that the first line is _not_ indented. [Example 1](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/4f9ae42d861fcb4be2fcd5d3d55d5f227d30e723/src/libraries/System.IO/tests/IndentedTextWriter.cs#L46-L67) and [Example 2](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/4f9ae42d861fcb4be2fcd5d3d55d5f227d30e723/src/libraries/System.IO/tests/IndentedTextWriter.cs#L74-L93).

The original issue #30351 points to [this Gist](https://gist.github.com/markrendle/01b8b70d2b7c4f023bf196e96562c14a) which shows there is still a bug in `WriteLineAsync` when printing a non-empty line after an empty line:

```
Foo
    Bar

Quux
    Wibble
```
- Foo is not indented (expected).
- Quux is not indented (unexpected).

You can verify the _correct_ behavior if you test the `WriteLine` method:

```cs
using System;
using System.CodeDom.Compiler;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {

        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        var sw = new StringWriter(sb);
        using var tw = new IndentedTextWriter(sw);

        tw.Indent++;

        tw.WriteLine("Foo");
        tw.WriteLine("Bar");
        tw.WriteLine();
        tw.WriteLine("Quux");
        tw.WriteLine("Wibble");

        Console.WriteLine(sb.ToString());
    }
}
```

Output:
```
Foo
    Bar

    Quux
    Wibble
```
- Foo is not indented (expected).
- Quux is indented (expected).

@samtrion If we want to avoid introducing a breaking change, we should ensure we are _not_ indenting the very first line that gets printed.

The only bug that should be fixed is the one where `WriteLineAsync` prints a non-empty line after printing an empty line (Quux in the examples), to match the behavior of `WriteLine`.
